orecast  and seeing  how if those winds are going to continue  um time is of the urgency now because  those winds are going to continue to  push the vessel  more and more horizontal we need to get  target tugs there as quick as possible  to  try and start putting her off try  straightening her up  um i believe they're looking at if they  can't do it quickly  bringing in dredges to start dredging  the sand round everything to try and get  her off  um you're fighting against nature at the  moment um the the the  with the position she's in the with the  streamings and the  the streamings of the water in the in  the canal will be pushing her against  pushing her more and more horizontal  that together with the wind  um you have to remember that these  vessels are essentially big wind  catchers because they're so big and so  square  the wind catches them very hard and as  long as that wind's blowing it's going  to be a very big  mission to try and pull her off looking  at a bit later into the chain  which industries do you think would be  most affected by delayed container ships  i think you'll find that most industries  are be affected for it um  i wouldn't necessarily say to rush out  and go and stock up of  of products in your supermarket but if  you look at the trade between europe and  asia  it is a major lifeline so many of our  products come from there  um everything from hardware to furniture  to  to vehicles um and there'll be a little  bit of everything on board so it's  it's just generally going to delay the  delivery of things to the  um to manufacturers to and to the end  client  um it depends very very heavily on how  long they take together  to get her off but you have to remember  at the moment you've got 50 ships a day  stock  stacking up behind her and looking at  the on the charts a short while ago you  can already  see ships starting to to pull up there  so it's going to cause congestion and  at a lot of ports coming in in a couple  of in the next cou